# modules/cloud/aws/live/checks-cognito.rules - the §8.3 Cognito read-only
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-20).  Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/cognito.sh; ids are all
# `CLOUD-COGNITO-*`.
#
# WHY THIS IS A PER-SERVICE FILE RATHER THAN A BLOCK APPENDED TO THE SHARED
# `checks.rules` BESIDE IT.  That file's own header reserves both spellings and
# names the condition under which the second is right: "A later service whose
# peers really are in flight simultaneously may add its own
# `checks-<service>.rules` beside this one; both spellings load, because
# `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) globs every `*.rules` under
# `modules/cloud/` at any depth with no per-file allowlist and no registration
# step."  Step 6's per-service tickets ARE in flight simultaneously - one PR per
# service, all cut from the same `dev` - so twenty-four records appended to one
# shared file is a guaranteed conflict for every peer that lands after it, and
# a conflict in a registry is resolved by hand-merging two blocks of records,
# which is where a duplicated or dropped `id` gets committed with a straight
# face.  rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table reserves the `checks-` PREFIX
# with the `.rules` extension repository-wide and at any depth, so this path is
# a §9.5 script-check registry by that row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29); the
# SUFFIX spelling `cognito-checks.rules` would still be `E070`.
#
# `coverage-scope: account-region` is CLOUD's cell (§9.5.1), and EVERY RECORD
# BELOW LANDS IN THE `<account_id>/<region>` CELL - never `<account_id>/global`.
# Cognito is a `regional` row in modules/cloud/aws/engine.sh's `_CLOUD_SERVICES`
# because both of its API namespaces are regional: a user pool created in
# eu-west-2 is invisible to `cognito-idp list-user-pools` in us-east-1, and the
# same is true of `cognito-identity list-identity-pools`.  That is the OPPOSITE
# of the `s3` row beside it, where `list-buckets` is one account-wide call and
# the bucket's own region has to be resolved per bucket and deliberately
# differs from the cell.  Here the pass visits one region at a time and every
# resource it can see is in that region by construction, so the cell and the
# finding's `loc_region` are the same value and cannot drift.
#
# TWENTY-FOUR IDS, AND EVERY SPLIT IS FORCED OR DELIBERATE.  The CLOUD location
# profile is (account_id, region, resource_key, sub_key) and carries no
# component naming the DEFECT, so two different problems on one pool - or on
# one app client - under one id would hash to one fingerprint and
# `findings_merge` would keep whichever sorted first.  That forces one id per
# defect.  Three pairs are split for the SECOND reason, which is that severity
# varies with what was observed and `severity` is a per-record field (§9.5)
# every suite asserts the script and the registry agree on - so a script that
# "weighted a finding higher" at runtime would put the two into disagreement:
#
#   MFA_OFF / MFA_OPTIONAL                 no MFA at all versus MFA the user
#                                          may decline.
#   CLIENT_INSECURE_CALLBACK /
#     CLIENT_WILDCARD_CALLBACK             a plaintext destination versus one
#                                          that is not a fixed destination at
#                                          all.
#   IDPOOL_UNAUTH_ROLE_ADMIN /
#     IDPOOL_UNAUTH_ROLE_BROAD             an anonymous role holding `*` on `*`
#                                          versus one holding a whole service.
#
# Two ids also keeps the ordinary and the escalated case two findings rather
# than one whose meaning flips between runs - the identical argument
# `modules/dast/passive/checks-markup.rules`'s two `DAST-MARKUP-TABNABBING*`
# ids and this directory's own two `CLOUD-S3-PUBLIC_ACL_*` ids both record.
#
# NOT ONE RECORD BELOW CARRIES A `cis:` VALUE, AND THAT IS AN HONEST ABSENCE
# RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.  data/cis-mappings declares its benchmark as CIS
# Amazon Web Services Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0, and that benchmark has NO
# Cognito section: its scope is IAM (section 1), Storage (2), Logging (3),
# Monitoring (4) and Networking (5), and no control in any of them is about a
# user pool, an app client or an identity pool.  Two neighbouring controls are
# close enough to be tempting and are both wrong:
#
#   1.8  "Ensure IAM password policy requires minimum length of 14 or greater"
#        governs the IAM ACCOUNT password policy, which applies to IAM
#        principals with console access to the AWS account.  A Cognito user
#        pool governs an APPLICATION'S END USERS and its policy is a different
#        object entirely; citing 1.8 against it would tell a compliance reader
#        that an IAM control was assessed when it was not.
#   1.10 "Ensure MFA is enabled for all IAM users that have a console password"
#        is the same category error applied to MFA_OFF.
#
# docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5 item 5 forbids exactly this misattribution, and this
# directory's own `checks.rules` already records the same decision from the
# other side: three of its seven S3 records cite nothing, because v3.0.0 has no
# S3 control for encryption-at-rest, versioning or access logging.  An honest
# absence is the right outcome - `cis` is `optional, repeatable` (§9.5), an
# unmapped finding still carries its CWE and OWASP category, and inventing a
# control number to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the overstated-coverage
# failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 exists to forbid.  A future ticket that
# transcribes a benchmark control genuinely covering one of these adds the row
# via docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5 and the `cis:` line here in the same change.
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record: each check reads a
# `describe-*`/`list-*`/`get-*` response and mutates nothing, so lib/checks.sh's
# `--intensity` ceiling passes all of them at the default `passive` (its own
# type table names `config-read` in the `passive` row).  They also carry
# `quick`, which docs/DESIGN.md §5 defines as "passive + config-read, no active
# probes" - omitting it would leave `--profile-scan quick` selecting no cognito
# check at all.  NONE carries `compliance`, which is finding F3's TAG reading
# of that profile (lib/checks.sh): the tag means "this check contributes to a
# compliance report", and with no `cis` value to contribute it would name a
# check the CIS view has nothing to say about.  None is `intrusive` either:
# every answer is config-derived, nothing here calls `SignUp`,
# `ForgotPassword`, `InitiateAuth` or `GetCredentialsForIdentity`, and `aws_ro`
# refuses a non-read operation with exit 3 before the CLI is even exec'd.

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-WEAK_PASSWORD_POLICY-01
title: Cognito user pool password policy is below the baseline
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-521
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Raise this user pool's password policy to at least an 8-character
  minimum with all four character classes required (uppercase, lowercase,
  numbers and symbols), which is the policy Cognito applies by default to a
  pool that configures none - a pool reported here has been explicitly
  loosened below that default. Length is the single most effective of the
  five settings; the character-class requirements chiefly raise the cost of
  offline cracking once a hash is stolen. Note that changing the policy
  affects new passwords and password resets only, so existing users keep
  whatever they set until they next change it - pair the change with a forced
  rotation if the current policy has been weak for long. Consider enabling
  advanced security mode ENFORCED alongside it, which additionally refuses a
  password Cognito has seen in a known-compromised credential list, something
  no complexity rule can do.
references: CWE-521
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-policies.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-TEMP_PASSWORD_VALIDITY-01
title: Cognito user pool temporary passwords stay valid for longer than the default
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-262
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Lower TemporaryPasswordValidityDays back towards Cognito's own
  7-day default. A temporary password is created by an administrator and
  delivered to the user over email or SMS, so for the whole of this window a
  working credential for the account is sitting in a mailbox or a message log -
  channels that are neither encrypted end to end nor under the operator's
  control. Widening the window does not make onboarding more reliable; it only
  lengthens the period in which a forwarded, archived or intercepted message is
  still a usable credential. If users genuinely cannot complete first sign-in
  within a week, re-issue the invitation rather than extending its life.
references: CWE-262
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-policies.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-MFA_OFF-01
title: Cognito user pool has multi-factor authentication switched off
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-308
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set this user pool's MFA configuration to ON (required) and
  enable at least one second factor. Prefer TOTP software tokens over SMS:
  SMS delivery is interceptable by SIM swap and by SS7 attacks on the carrier
  network, so it raises the cost of an account takeover without bounding it.
  Turning MFA ON for a pool that already has users forces every one of them
  through enrolment at next sign-in, so plan the rollout - but note that the
  intermediate setting, OPTIONAL, leaves the decision with each user and in
  practice means most accounts have no second factor at all. If a phased
  rollout is genuinely required, pair OPTIONAL with advanced security mode
  ENFORCED so that at least the highest-risk sign-ins are challenged, and
  treat that as a temporary state with a date on it.
references: CWE-308
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-mfa.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-MFA_OPTIONAL-01
title: Cognito user pool leaves multi-factor authentication optional
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-308
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Move this user pool from OPTIONAL to ON so a second factor is
  required rather than offered. OPTIONAL is reported separately from OFF, and
  more mildly, because the mechanism is configured and some users are
  protected by it - but it is not a control the operator can rely on, since
  whether any given account has a second factor is that user's decision and
  the answer for most accounts is no. An attacker with a valid password does
  not need to defeat the second factor; they need only find an account that
  never enrolled one. Before switching to ON, check how many users have
  actually enrolled, and pair the change with a supported enrolment path so
  the cutover does not lock out the accounts that had not.
references: CWE-308
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-mfa.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-ADVANCED_SECURITY_OFF-01
title: Cognito user pool advanced security is not enforced
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1173
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set this user pool's advanced security mode to ENFORCED. In
  ENFORCED mode Cognito refuses a sign-in that presents a password it has seen
  in a known-compromised credential list, and applies adaptive
  authentication - blocking or challenging a sign-in whose risk score is high
  because of the device, the location or the pattern of attempts. Neither
  behaviour exists in the two other modes. AUDIT is reported here alongside
  OFF, and the distinction matters when you act on this finding rather than
  when you triage it: in AUDIT mode Cognito computes exactly the same risk
  score and publishes it to CloudWatch, and then takes no action on it, so a
  sign-in with a credential known to be in a breach corpus succeeds exactly as
  it would with the feature off. AUDIT is a reasonable place to sit for a
  short, dated observation period before switching to ENFORCED; it is not a
  control. Note advanced security is a paid feature billed per monthly active
  user, so budget for it rather than being surprised by it.
references: CWE-1173
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pool-settings-advanced-security.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-SELF_REGISTRATION_OPEN-01
title: Cognito user pool allows unauthenticated self-registration
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1390
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Confirm that open self-registration is intended for this pool. It
  is reported for review rather than as a defect, because for a
  consumer-facing application it is the whole point of having a user pool -
  but for an internal or B2B pool it means anyone on the internet can create
  an account, and every downstream authorization decision that assumes "a
  signed-in user is a known user" is then wrong. If it is not intended, set
  AdminCreateUserConfig.AllowAdminCreateUserOnly to true so accounts are
  created only by an administrator or by a provisioning integration. If it is
  intended, make sure the application does not treat mere authentication as
  authorization: gate anything privileged on a group, a claim or a record the
  self-registered user cannot set for themselves, and check the app clients'
  writable attributes (this pack's CLIENT_WRITABLE_ATTRIBUTE check) so a new
  account cannot assert its own verification state or role.
references: CWE-1390
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/signing-up-users-in-your-app.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-RECOVERY_SMS_ONLY-01
title: Cognito user pool account recovery prefers SMS over email
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-640
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Reorder this pool's account-recovery mechanisms so that
  verified_email sits at priority 1 and any phone-based mechanism below it, or
  set recovery to admin_only if self-service recovery is not wanted at all.
  Account recovery is an authentication bypass by design - it hands whoever
  controls the channel a way into the account without the password - so the
  channel it prefers is as security-relevant as the password policy itself. A
  phone number is not under the account holder's sole control: a SIM-swap
  against the carrier, a ported number, or an SS7 interception all give an
  attacker the recovery code, and none of them requires touching the user's
  device. This is reported on the HIGHEST-PRIORITY mechanism rather than on
  membership, so a pool that offers email first and phone as a fallback is not
  flagged - that is a reasonable configuration, and the finding is specifically
  about SMS being the path a recovery attempt takes by default.
references: CWE-640
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/how-to-recover-a-user-account.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-DELETION_PROTECTION_OFF-01
title: Cognito user pool deletion protection is inactive
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set DeletionProtection to ACTIVE on this user pool. With it
  inactive, a single DeleteUserPool call - from a mistaken console click, a
  Terraform plan that recreates the resource, or a credential an attacker
  holds - destroys every user account, every password and every attribute in
  the pool, and there is no undo and no export to restore from. Cognito offers
  no backup of a user pool's directory, so this is one of the few AWS
  resources whose loss is genuinely unrecoverable rather than merely
  disruptive. Deletion protection costs nothing, does not affect any runtime
  behaviour, and requires only that the pool be updated to disable it before a
  legitimate deletion - which is precisely the confirmation step that is
  missing today.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-deletion-protection.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-SELF_SERVICE_SURFACE-01
title: Cognito user pool exposes unauthenticated self-service operations
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1059
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Review the listed operations against what this application is
  meant to offer anonymously. This is an inventory rather than a defect: every
  operation named here is a legitimate Cognito feature and a great many
  applications intend to expose all of them. What it gives you is one place to
  see which anonymous entry points the pool actually has, derived from the
  pool's own configuration rather than by calling them - scoursh never sends
  SignUp or ForgotPassword, because doing so creates real accounts and fires
  real verification email and SMS to whatever address the probe invented. Each
  operation is reachable by anyone who holds an app client id, which is a
  public value that ships in the application's own JavaScript or mobile
  binary, so treat each as an internet-facing endpoint: confirm the pool's
  rate limits are adequate, confirm PreventUserExistenceErrors is ENABLED on
  every client so these endpoints cannot be used to enumerate accounts, and
  confirm that a self-registered or self-recovered account is not implicitly
  trusted anywhere downstream.
references: CWE-1059
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-identity-pools.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_PLAINTEXT_AUTH-01
title: Cognito app client permits a non-SRP password authentication flow
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-522
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the plaintext password flows from this app client's
  ExplicitAuthFlows and use ALLOW_USER_SRP_AUTH instead. Under SRP the client
  proves knowledge of the password through a zero-knowledge exchange and the
  password itself never leaves the device; under ALLOW_USER_PASSWORD_AUTH,
  ALLOW_ADMIN_USER_PASSWORD_AUTH or their legacy spellings the password is
  sent to Cognito as a request parameter. TLS protects it on the wire, so this
  is not an interception finding - the exposure is everywhere the request is
  handled in cleartext at the endpoints: an application log that records
  request bodies, an APM or error-reporting agent that captures parameters, a
  proxy or WAF doing TLS termination and logging, and any backend that relays
  the call and might retain it. Migrating to SRP removes the whole class
  rather than any single instance of it. Where a flow genuinely cannot be
  migrated, at minimum confirm that nothing in the request path logs bodies,
  and treat that as a compensating control with an owner rather than a fix.
references: CWE-522
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/amazon-cognito-user-pools-authentication-flow.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_IMPLICIT_OAUTH-01
title: Cognito app client permits the implicit OAuth grant
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-598
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove `implicit` from this app client's AllowedOAuthFlows and
  use the authorization code grant with PKCE instead. In the implicit grant
  Cognito returns the access and ID tokens in the URL FRAGMENT of the redirect,
  so the tokens land in the browser address bar, in the session history, in
  any referrer a subsequent navigation sends, and in whatever the browser or
  an extension records - none of which is a place a bearer token can be
  revoked from. The code grant returns a single-use authorization code instead
  and exchanges it for tokens over a direct back-channel request, and PKCE
  binds that exchange to the client that started it so an intercepted code is
  useless. The implicit grant was deprecated by OAuth 2.0 Security Best
  Current Practice for exactly these reasons and is not appropriate for new
  clients; every current Cognito SDK supports code+PKCE, including from a
  single-page application, so there is rarely a migration blocker beyond the
  redirect handling itself.
references: CWE-598
references: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-app-idp-settings.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_INSECURE_CALLBACK-01
title: Cognito app client has a plaintext HTTP callback or logout URL
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Change this app client's callback and logout URLs to https://.
  The callback URL is where Cognito delivers an authorization code or - with
  the implicit grant - the tokens themselves, so a plaintext destination means
  that delivery crosses the network unprotected: anyone on the path can read
  the code or the token and use it, and anyone who can inject can redirect the
  user somewhere else entirely. A loopback URL (http://localhost or
  http://127.0.0.1) is NOT reported here, because RFC 8252 section 7.3
  endorses it for native applications - the redirect never leaves the user's
  own machine - so anything this check names is a real network destination.
  Note that Cognito's console refuses a plaintext non-loopback URL today, so a
  client carrying one was created through the API or predates that validation:
  check whether it is still in use at all before spending effort migrating it.
references: CWE-319
references: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-app-settings.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_WILDCARD_CALLBACK-01
title: Cognito app client has a wildcarded or hostless callback or logout URL
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-601
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace every wildcarded or hostless entry in this app client's
  callback and logout URL lists with the exact URLs the application actually
  uses. The redirect URI allow-list is the only thing standing between an
  authorization flow and an attacker-chosen destination: a wildcard host such
  as https://*.example.com admits every subdomain, including one an attacker
  obtained through a dangling DNS record or a compromised sub-site, and a
  wildcard path admits any handler on the host. An attacker who can name the
  redirect can start an authorization flow, have the victim complete it, and
  receive the authorization code or token at a destination they control - a
  full account takeover that leaves no trace in the application itself. This
  is rated above the plaintext-callback finding because a wildcard is
  exploitable by anyone who can register or take over one matching name,
  whereas plaintext needs network position. Enumerate the real redirect URIs
  from the application's own configuration rather than widening the list to
  make an environment work; if several environments share a client, give each
  its own client instead.
references: CWE-601
references: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-app-settings.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_TOKEN_LIFETIME-01
title: Cognito app client configures an excessive token lifetime
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-613
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Lower the token validity settings named in this finding. A
  Cognito access or ID token is a bearer JWT that Cognito itself cannot
  invalidate before it expires - there is no revocation list and no
  introspection endpoint - so the lifetime is a hard floor on how long a stolen
  token keeps working after the theft is discovered, the user's password is
  changed, or the account is disabled. The access and ID tokens are reported
  above 12 hours, which is the midpoint of Cognito's own permitted range and
  twelve times its own default, so a client named here has been pushed most of
  the way to the 24-hour ceiling rather than merely being generous; an
  hour is the default and is right for most applications. A refresh token IS
  revocable (see this pack's CLIENT_TOKEN_REVOCATION_OFF check), but only if
  revocation is enabled and something actually calls it, so a multi-year
  refresh token on a client with revocation off is an unbounded session. Set
  the refresh window to the longest period a user should stay signed in
  without re-authenticating, not to the longest the API accepts.
references: CWE-613
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/amazon-cognito-user-pools-using-tokens-with-identity-providers.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_TOKEN_REVOCATION_OFF-01
title: Cognito app client has token revocation disabled
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-613
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable token revocation on this app client. Without it there is
  no way to end a session before its tokens expire: the RevokeToken API
  refuses to act for a client that has it disabled, so a refresh token stolen
  from a device, a browser profile or a log continues to mint fresh access
  tokens for its whole validity window regardless of anything the operator
  does. Password change, account disable and even user deletion do not stop
  it. Enabling revocation is a one-field change with no effect on existing
  sign-ins, and it is what makes a sign-out actually sign the user out rather
  than only clearing local storage. Having enabled it, make sure the
  application calls RevokeToken on sign-out and on any security-relevant
  event - the capability alone changes nothing until something uses it - and
  review this client's refresh token lifetime at the same time, since the two
  settings together decide the worst-case exposure of a stolen session.
references: CWE-613
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/token-revocation.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_WRITABLE_ATTRIBUTE-01
title: Cognito app client may write a security-relevant user attribute
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the named attributes from this app client's
  WriteAttributes list. An attribute the client may write is one the END USER
  may write, because the client is the user's own browser or mobile
  application and every request it makes is under their control - so a
  writable email_verified or phone_number_verified lets any account assert
  that it has verified an address it does not own, and a writable custom
  privilege attribute lets it assert its own role, group, plan or
  entitlement. Wherever the application, a Lambda trigger, an API Gateway
  authorizer or a downstream service reads that claim to make an authorization
  decision, this is a direct privilege escalation available to anyone who can
  register an account. Attributes that reflect a decision the system makes
  about a user belong in a group membership or in a record the user cannot
  reach, never in a self-asserted attribute; only genuinely
  user-owned preferences belong in WriteAttributes. This check is medium
  confidence for the custom-attribute half alone: it matches a short list of
  names that are privileges in effectively every application that uses them,
  and cannot know what a given application's custom attribute means, so
  confirm the named attribute's role before acting.
references: CWE-269
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-attributes.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_USER_EXISTENCE_ERRORS-01
title: Cognito app client reveals whether a username exists
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-204
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set PreventUserExistenceErrors to ENABLED on this app client.
  With it LEGACY - which is also the effective setting for any client created
  before the option existed - Cognito answers UserNotFoundException for an
  unknown username and NotAuthorizedException for a known username with the
  wrong password, so anyone holding the app client id can tell the two apart
  one request at a time and enumerate the pool's entire membership. The client
  id is a public value that ships in the application's own JavaScript or
  mobile binary, so no credential is needed to do this. What the enumeration
  buys an attacker is a validated target list: which of a leaked
  email/password corpus applies here, whose addresses to phish with a
  convincing "your account" message, and which accounts to spray a common
  password against. Enabling the setting makes both responses identical, and
  also removes the same oracle from the forgot-password and confirmation
  flows. It is a per-client setting, so check every client on the pool -
  scoursh reports each separately.
references: CWE-204
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-managing-errors.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-CLIENT_PUBLIC_CONFIDENTIAL_FLOW-01
title: Cognito public app client permits a flow that assumes a client secret
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-287
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Either give this app client a secret and move it behind a
  backend that can keep one, or remove the named flows from it. A client with
  no secret is a PUBLIC client - a single-page application or a mobile binary
  whose code an attacker holds a copy of - so anything the client is permitted
  to do, anyone is permitted to do. The client_credentials grant authenticates
  the CLIENT ITSELF with no user in the exchange at all, so its entire security
  rests on the secret being secret; offered by a public client it is an
  unauthenticated path to whatever scopes it carries. The ADMIN_* authentication
  flows are meant to be called server-to-server by a trusted backend, and they
  bypass parts of the user-facing flow accordingly. Create a separate
  confidential client for the server-side work and leave the public client with
  ALLOW_USER_SRP_AUTH and the authorization code grant with PKCE, which is the
  combination designed for a client that cannot keep a secret.
references: CWE-287
references: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/user-pool-settings-client-apps.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_UNAUTH_IDENTITIES-01
title: Cognito identity pool allows unauthenticated identities
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-306
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Turn off AllowUnauthenticatedIdentities on this identity pool
  unless anonymous access is a deliberate, documented requirement. With it on,
  any caller who knows the identity pool id - a public value that ships in the
  application's own JavaScript or mobile binary - can obtain an identity from
  the pool with no credential of any kind. On its own that is a configuration
  fact rather than an exposure, which is why this is reported separately from
  and more mildly than the two findings that follow it: what turns it into an
  exposure is an unauthenticated IAM role being attached to the pool, and what
  turns that into a serious one is the scope of that role. Look for
  CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_UNAUTH_CREDENTIALS-01 and the two
  IDPOOL_UNAUTH_ROLE_* findings on this same pool before deciding how urgent
  this is. If anonymous access IS required - the usual legitimate case is a
  public read of an S3 bucket or a Pinpoint analytics event before sign-in -
  keep it, and make the unauthenticated role as narrow as that one use.
references: CWE-306
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/identity-pools.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_UNAUTH_CREDENTIALS-01
title: Cognito identity pool hands AWS credentials to unauthenticated callers
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-306
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: This identity pool both allows unauthenticated identities AND has
  an unauthenticated IAM role attached, so GetCredentialsForIdentity will
  return real, signed AWS credentials for that role to any caller who knows the
  pool id - no sign-in, no token, no account. The pool id is not a secret: it
  ships in the application's own JavaScript or mobile binary and is routinely
  visible in a browser's network tab. Decide first whether anonymous access is
  required at all; if it is not, turn off AllowUnauthenticatedIdentities, which
  removes the whole path. If it is, treat the unauthenticated role exactly as
  you would a set of AWS credentials published on the internet: grant it the
  single action on the single resource the pre-sign-in experience needs,
  nothing more, and add a condition on aws:PrincipalTag or on the identity
  pool's own audience so the role cannot be assumed from another pool. Note
  that scoursh derives this from the pool's configuration and never calls
  GetCredentialsForIdentity - the credentials are obtainable, and no scan
  obtained any.
references: CWE-306
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/identity-pools-roles.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_UNAUTH_ROLE_ADMIN-01
title: Cognito identity pool unauthenticated role holds unrestricted permissions
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Rewrite or detach this policy now. The unauthenticated role of an
  identity pool that allows anonymous identities is assumable by anyone who
  knows the pool id, which is a public value shipped in the application's own
  code - so a policy statement on it granting Action "*" on Resource "*", or
  using NotAction/NotResource to the same effect, is administrative access to
  the AWS account published on the internet. Anyone who reads the application's
  JavaScript can read every bucket, every secret and every database in the
  account, create and destroy resources, and escalate further through IAM.
  Treat this as an active incident rather than a hardening item: assume the
  permissions have been used, review CloudTrail for GetCredentialsForIdentity
  calls and for the role's own session activity, then replace the policy with
  the single action on the single resource the pre-sign-in experience needs. If
  nothing needs anonymous access at all, turn off
  AllowUnauthenticatedIdentities, which removes the path entirely. scoursh
  reports the wildcard grant only where the statement carries NO Condition, so
  what it names here is unconditioned by the policy itself.
references: CWE-269
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/identity-pools-roles.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_UNAUTH_ROLE_BROAD-01
title: Cognito identity pool unauthenticated role holds a service-wide wildcard
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Narrow this policy to the specific actions and resources the
  pre-sign-in experience needs. The unauthenticated role of an identity pool
  that allows anonymous identities is assumable by anyone who knows the pool
  id - a public value shipped in the application's own code - so a statement
  granting a whole service (an Action of the form service:* against a Resource
  of "*") hands every anonymous caller every operation that service offers,
  across every resource in the account. For s3:* that is read, write and delete
  on every bucket; for dynamodb:* it is every table; for lambda:* it is
  invoking and reconfiguring every function. This is reported below the
  unrestricted-admin finding because it is bounded by one service rather than
  by nothing, and it is still an anonymous grant. Replace the wildcard with the
  named actions (typically a single s3:GetObject or
  mobiletargeting:PutEvents) and scope Resource to the exact ARN prefix, then
  review CloudTrail for use of the role before assuming it was never
  exercised.
references: CWE-269
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/identity-pools-roles.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_CLASSIC_FLOW-01
title: Cognito identity pool has the classic (basic) authentication flow enabled
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set AllowClassicFlow to false on this identity pool. The classic
  flow re-enables Cognito Identity's original two-step credential exchange -
  GetOpenIdToken followed by a direct sts assume-role-with-web-identity call -
  alongside the modern single-step GetCredentialsForIdentity. The two-step form
  hands the caller an OpenID token they then present to STS themselves, which
  takes role selection out of the identity pool's own role-mapping rules and
  lets the caller name the role: a principal who can obtain the token can
  attempt to assume any role whose trust policy accepts the pool, not only the
  one the pool would have chosen for them. It also widens the window for token
  replay, since the intermediate token is a separate credential with its own
  lifetime. The classic flow exists for compatibility with clients written
  before the enhanced flow; unless one of those is still in use, turning it off
  removes the alternative path with no effect on current SDKs. If it must stay
  on, audit every role whose trust policy names this identity pool.
references: CWE-1104
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/authentication-flow.html

id: CLOUD-COGNITO-IDPOOL_ROLE_MAPPING-01
title: Cognito identity pool role mapping falls back to the authenticated role
script: aws/live/cognito.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-863
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Change AmbiguousRoleResolution from AuthenticatedRole to Deny for
  the named role mappings. A role mapping exists to give different classes of
  user different IAM roles based on a claim in their token;
  AmbiguousRoleResolution decides what happens when a token matches several
  rules or none of them. AuthenticatedRole falls back to the pool's DEFAULT
  authenticated role, so a token carrying an unexpected claim - or a claim the
  token's issuer lets the user influence, which for a self-registered Cognito
  user with a writable custom attribute is common - silently receives whatever
  that default role can do, rather than being refused. The two settings are
  easy to read the wrong way round: Deny is the safe value and AuthenticatedRole
  is the permissive one, despite the names. Set Deny, then confirm the rules
  actually cover every legitimate class of user, because with Deny in place a
  gap in the rules becomes a visible failure instead of a silent grant - which
  is the point.
references: CWE-863
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/role-based-access-control.html
